s cryptographic proofs of neural network forward passes using sumchecks and logup GKR, achieving sublinear proving time in model size — orders of magnitude faster than circuit-based approaches.\nL222: \nL223: Confirmed working models: GPT-2, Gemma 3, Llama 2 — all transformer layers proven end-to-end, from token embeddings through to next-token argmax. MLP and CNN inference is also supported.\nL224: This repository is a Rust workspace. The 【103†`zkml`】 crate is the core proving library; the remaining crates provide the client stack, storage layer, and developer tooling.\nL225: ## Headline Numbers\nL226: \nL227: Single-machine inference proving on a 24-core / 504 GB CPU server:\nL228: \nL229: Model  | Sequence  | Prove time  | Verify  | Proof size  | Throughput\nL230: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL231: GPT-2  | 512 tokens  | 7.6 min  | 1.3 s  | 10.7 MiB  | 1.12 tokens/s (67 tokens/min)\nL232: Gemma 3  | 512 tokens  | 19 min  | 4.3 s  | 27 MiB  | 0.45 tokens/s (27 tokens/min)\nL233:   * 10–30× faster than the previous published state of the art (e.g. zkGPT reports ≈ 0.05 tokens/s on similar hardware).\nL234:   * Accuracy preserved: ≥99.6% cosine similarity to the floating-point baseline at 12-bit quantization (GPT-2).\nL235:   * Scales out: horizontal proof distribution and GPU acceleration are supported today; clusters of GPU workers are on the roadmap.\nL236: For the full methodology and a deeper benchmark sweep across sequence lengths and models, see the DeepProve paper (link to be added) and 【102†`zkml/README.md`】.\nL237: ## Repository Structure\nL238: Crate  | Description\nL239: --- | ---\nL240: 【103†`zkml`】  | Core proving library — model quantization, layer implementations (MLP, CNN, transformer), and ZK proof generation/verification\nL241: 【104†`deep-prove`】  | Client stack — `deep-prove-worker` runs a proof generation server; `deep-prove-cli` submits proving jobs locally or to a remote proving network\nL242: 【105†`tenstore`】  | Storage facade for persisting and retrieving tensor data; supports local and remote (S3-compatible) backends\nL243: 【106†`tenvis`】  | Interactive CLI tool for inspecting and debugging proof data stored in tenstore\nL244: 【107†`telemetry`】  | Shared OpenTelemetry tracing and logging setup used across all crates\nL245: 【108†`utils`】  | Shared utility helpers: CSV recording, memory tracking, statistical summaries\nL246: ## Licensing\nL247: \nL248: Licensed under the 【89†Lagrange License】.\nL249: \nL250: ## Acknowledgements\nL251: \nL252: This project builds upon the work from 【109†scroll-tech/ceno】, reusing the sumcheck and GKR implementation from that codebase.\nL253: \nL254: ## About\nL255: \nL256: Framework to prove inference of ML models blazingly fast\nL257: \nL258: 【55†lagrange.dev†lagrange.dev】\nL259: \nL260: ### Topics\nL261: \nL262: 【56†ai】【57†ml】【58†zk】【59†zk-snarks】【60†zkml】\nL263: \nL264: ### Resources\nL265: \nL266: 【61†Readme】\nL267: \nL268: 【62†License】\nL269: \nL270: 【63†Activity】\nL271: \nL272: 【64†Custom properties】\nL273: \nL274: ### Stars\nL275: \nL276: 3.4k stars\nL277: \nL278: ### Watchers\nL279: \nL280: 17 watching\nL281: ### Forks\nL282: \nL283: 【65†104 forks】\nL284: \nL285: 【66†Report repository】\nL286: \nL287: ## Releases\nL288: \nL289: ## Packages\nL290: \nL291: ## Used by\nL292: \nL293: ## Contributors\nL294: \nL295: ## Languages\nL296: \nL297: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDB-SpMSpV: Dual-View Blocked Sparse Matrix-Sparse Vector Multiplication for Dynamic GPU Workloads (https://arxiv.org/html/2608.16308v1)\nciteturn4view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2608.16308v1\",\"lineno\":400}); Total lines: 512\nL380: For each matrix, we generate random input vectors at different sparsity levels with a fixed seed of 42. We compare DB-SpMSpV with general-purpose cuSPARSE and specialized CB-SpMV and TileSpMSpV; for fairness, cuSPARSE BSR uses the same block size as DB-SpMSpV. All kernels are warmed up and executed 100 times, and performance is reported in GFLOPS using the SpMV floating-point operation count.\nL381: Fig.【85†8】 shows that DB-SpMSpV achieves the best performance on both GPUs for most matrices, with a larger advantage as input sparsity decreases from 0.1 to 0.0001. On A100, DB-SpMSpV obtains average speedups of 5.48$\\times$–64.34$\\times$, 1.20$\\times$–8.29$\\times$, and 2.36$\\times$–14.01$\\times$ over cuSPARSE, CB-SpMV, and TileSpMSpV, respectively. On RTX 4090, the corresponding speedups are 8.86$\\times$–128.46$\\times$, 1.29$\\times$–6.98$\\times$, and 2.72$\\times$–12.04$\\times$.\nL382: The increasing advantage at lower input sparsities is consistent with DB-SpMSpV’s vector-block masks and adaptive push/pull execution: inactive regions can be skipped when the vector is sparse, while denser vector blocks can use pull-style computation to improve memory regularity and reduce writeback conflicts.\nL383: ### 5.3. Application Performance\nL384: Table 2. Performance comparison of different linear projections. Time is reported in us, with speedup shown in parentheses.\nL385: Model  | Q  | K  | V  | Out  | Up  | Down\nL386: PyTorch  | Ours  | PyTorch  | Ours  | PyTorch  | Ours  | PyTorch  | Ours  | PyTorch  | Ours  | PyTorch  | Ours\nL387: Llama-3.2-1B  | 6.53  | 2.69 (2.43$\\times$)  | 6.08  | 2.46 (2.47$\\times$)  | 6.08  | 2.46 (2.47$\\times$)  | 6.52  | 2.64 (2.47$\\times$)  | 14.96  | 4.38 (3.42$\\times$)  | 13.12  | 4.26 (3.08$\\times$)\nL388: Qwen2.5-1.5B  | 6.02  | 2.56 (2.35$\\times$)  | 6.00  | 2.44 (2.46$\\times$)  | 6.00  | 2.44 (2.46$\\times$)  | 6.03  | 2.54 (2.37$\\times$)  | 13.34  | 4.08 (3.27$\\times$)  | 19.24  | 4.27 (4.50$\\times$)\nL389: Qwen3-0.6B  | 6.04  | 2.54 (2.38$\\times$)  | 6.13  | 2.45 (2.50$\\times$)  | 6.00  | 2.46 (2.44$\\times$)  | 6.05  | 2.52 (2.40$\\times$)  | 6.14  | 2.60 (2.36$\\times$)  | 7.03  | 2.79 (2.52$\\times$)\nL390: Table 3. Performance comparison of BFS on representative matrices. Time is reported in ms, and speedup is shown in parentheses.\nL391: Metric  | pwtk  | pkustk14  | audikw_1  | crankseg_2  | halfb  | msdoor  | consph  | m_t1\nL392: Thumbnail  | 【86†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【87†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【88†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【89†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【90†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【91†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【92†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【93†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】\nL393: Row (Column) NNZs  | 217,918 11,524,432  | 151,926 14,836,504  | 943,695 77,651,847  | 22,283 24,669,643  | 224,617 12,387,821  | 415,863 19,173,163  | 83,334 6,010,480  | 97,578 9,753,570\nL394: Frontier Sparsity  | 【94†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【95†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【96†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【97†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【98†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【99†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【100†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】  | 【101†Image: [Uncaptioned image]】\nL395: Gunrock  | 10.87 (4.30$\\times$)  | 6.317 (5.31$\\times$)  | 12.49 (4.04$\\times$)  | 4.814 (9.47$\\times$)  | 6.577 (5.21$\\times$)  | 6.45 (4.01$\\times$)  | 3.094 (8.92$\\times$)  | 4.216 (8.58$\\times$)\nL396: Enterprise  | 30.856 (12.22$\\times$)  | 6.292 (5.29$\\times$)  | 4.26 (1.38$\\times$)  | 1.574 (3.10$\\times$)  | 8.354 (6.62$\\times$)  | 8.527 (5.30$\\times$)  | 2.10 (6.05$\\times$)  | 3.013 (6.13$\\times$)\nL397: GSwitch  | 10.299 (4.08$\\times$)  | 12.809 (10.76$\\times$)  | 66.33 (21.43$\\times$)  | 7.905 (15.56$\\times$)  | 5.849 (4.63$\\times$)  | 5.443 (3.38$\\times$)  | 1.453 (4.19$\\times$)  | 7.005 (14.25$\\times$)\nL398: TileBFS  | 8.758 (3.47$\\times$)  | 3.119 (2.62$\\times$)  | 3.490 (1.13$\\times$)  | 0.642 (1.26$\\times$)  | 3.715 (2.94$\\times$)  | 4.261 (2.65$\\times$)  | 0.889 (2.56$\\times$)  | 1.563 (3.18$\\times$)\nL399: DB-BFS-Pull  | 3.434 (1.36$\\times$)  | 1.908 (1.60$\\times$)  | 4.077 (1.32$\\times$)  | 1.027 (2.02$\\times$)  | 3.329 (2.64$\\times$)  | 3.617 (2.25$\\times$)  | 0.921 (2.65$\\times$)  | 1.133 (2.30$\\times$)\nL400: DB-BFS-Push  | 2.802 (1.11$\\times$)  | 1.191 (1.00$\\times$)  | 4.066 (1.31$\\times$)  | 0.861 (1.69$\\times$)  | 2.201 (1.74$\\times$)  | 2.553 (1.59$\\times$)  | 0.684 (1.97$\\times$)  | 0.849 (1.73$\\times$)\nL401: DB-BFS-Auto  | 2.526 (1.00$\\times$)  | 1.190 (1.00$\\times$)  | 3.095 (1.00$\\times$)  | 0.508 (1.00$\\times$)  | 1.262 (1.00$\\times$)  | 1.609 (1.00$\\times$)  | 0.347 (1.00$\\times$)  | 0.492 (1.00$\\times$)\nL402: We next evaluate whether the kernel-level advantages carry over to complete applications.\nL403: For BFS, we run single-source BFS from vertex 0 for all methods to ensure a fair comparison. Although the source is fixed, the selected matrices cover diverse graph structures, including finite-element meshes, banded graphs, and irregular networks, leading to varied frontier evolution patterns that are representative of typical BFS workloads. This end-to-end measurement includes frontier generation, visited-state maintenance, path switching, and multi-iteration control overheads.\nL404: On A100, DB-BFS achieves average speedups of 26.74$\\times$, 13.36$\\times$, 8.81$\\times$, and 2.66$\\times$ over Gunrock, Enterprise, GSwitch, and TileBFS, respectively. On RTX 4090, the corresponding speedups are 49.43$\\times$, 12.40$\\times$, 13.56$\\times$, and 3.60$\\times$. These gains come from representing frontiers directly at the block level, allowing sparse levels to avoid invalid edge scans through push and expanded levels to reduce writeback conflicts through pull.\nL405: For sparse decoding, we test Llama-3.2-1B(【102†Grattafiori et al., 2024】), Qwen2.5-1.5B(【103†Yang et al., 2025b】), and Qwen3-0.6B(【104†Yang et al., 2025a】). We apply Wanda(【105†Sun et al., 2024】) to prune 50% of the weights in each linear layer, while activation sparsity comes from magnitude-based thresholding(【106†Liu et al., 2025】), yielding about 10% activation density during single-token decoding. Tab.【107†2】 reports the average per-token decoding time of the corresponding layers.\nL406: DB-Decoding achieves consistent speedups across models and layers: Q/K/V/Out projections obtain about 2.35$\\times$–2.50$\\times$, while FFN projections show larger gains, up to 3.42$\\times$ for Up and 4.50$\\times$ for Down. Since weight matrices are reused after a single DB-format conversion and only activation masks change at runtime, DB-Decoding avoids repeatedly processing zero activations and zero-weight entries without maintaining multiple sparse weight layouts.\nL407: ### 5.4. Representative Analysis & Ablations\nL408: \nL409: We use representative matrices to examine more closely when adaptive choices matter.\nL410: For SpMSpV, we compare three low-level block storage strategies: DB-SpMSpV-COO, DB-SpMSpV-CSR, and DB-SpMSpV-Auto. Fig.【108†10】 shows that no fixed block format is consistently optimal across matrices. For example, pkustk14 benefits more from the COO-like format, achieving 157.538 GFLOPS compared with 115.855 GFLOPS for the CSR-like format, while crankseg_2 favors the CSR-like format, reaching 163.85 GFLOPS compared with 86.75 GFLOPS for the COO-like format.\nL411: DB-SpMSpV-Auto avoids this format-specific behavior and achieves the best performance on all representative matrices, reaching 201.353, 193.134, 182.02, and 338.129 GFLOPS on pwtk, pkustk14, crankseg_2, and consph, respectively. In particular, Auto improves over COO-like by more than 2.0$\\times$ on crankseg_2 and over CSR-like by about 1.67$\\times$ on pkustk14.\nL412: The contrast indicates that intra-block nonzero distribution and vector-block activity jointly shape the best local format, rather than either factor alone.\nL413: 【109†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 10. Ablation of low-level block storage formats in DB-SpMSpV. The input vector sparsity is 0.1, and all variants use the high-level pull computation path.\nL414: For BFS, Tab.【71†3】 reports representative matrices together with frontier evolution and end-to-end execution time. DB-BFS-Auto achieves the best performance on all representative matrices. On audikw_1, where the frontier first expands and then shrinks, DB-BFS-Auto takes 3.095ms, outperforming Gunrock at 12.49ms, GSwitch at 66.33ms, and TileBFS at 3.49ms.\nL415: On halfb, where the frontier is concentrated, and the matrix has a banded structure, DB-BFS-Auto reaches 1.262ms and still achieves a 2.94$\\times$ speedup over TileBFS.\nL416: The single-path variants show the same pattern from the application side. On halfb, DB-BFS-Auto takes 1.262ms, outperforming DB-BFS-Push at 2.201ms and DB-BFS-Pull at 3.329ms. On msdoor and consph, Auto also outperforms the better single-path variant. BFS frontiers move through different sparsity regimes across iterations, so a fixed direction either wastes scans in sparse stages or increases writeback pressure in denser stages. DB-BFS avoids this by changing direction with the frontier state.\nL417: ### 5.5. Overhead\nL418: Supporting both push and pull with full CSR+CSC duplication nearly doubles storage. By keeping only high-level dual views and reusing one low-level block payload, DB-SpMSpV saves 60.64%–61.68% storage over CSR+CSC in Tab.【110†4】, with an average saving of 61.26%.\nL419: It also uses less memory than plain CSR on these matrices because low-level block formats reduce local indexing overhead: COO blocks pack local coordinates, Dense blocks remove per-element indices, ForceCSR/ForceCSC encode local structure with masks, and patched CSR uses compact row/mapping fields. Together, these designs reduce the amortized index footprint below the 32-bit column index used by standard CSR.\nL420: The DB-format conversion is performed offline and reused across SpMSpV calls, BFS iterations, and decoding tokens; online overhead mainly comes from vector-mask generation, nonzero-block counting, path selection, and GPU kernel execution.\nL421: Table 4. Memory overhead comparison.\nL422: Method  | pwtk  | halfb  | consph  | m_t1\nL423: CSR (MB)  | 133.98  | 142.62  | 69.10  | 111.99\nL424: CSR+CSC (MB)  | 267.95  | 285.25  | 138.20  | 223.99\nL425: Ours (MB)  | 102.67  | 110.31  | 54.39  | 86.48\nL426: Memory Save (%)  | 61.68  | 61.33  | 60.64  | 61.39\nL427: ## 6. Related Work\nL428: \nL429: Prior work has improved sparse matrix computation from three closely related directions: storage formats, SpMSpV kernels, and graph applications.\nL430: Sparse blocked formats. CSR/CSC remain the basic row- and column-oriented sparse formats, and many SpMV systems improve their locality, load balance, or format selection.\nL431: CSR5 uses segmented tile descriptors for balanced SpMV, SMAT selects formats through input-adaptive tuning, and recent tiled/block formats improve GPU locality and regularity for SpMV-like kernels(【42†Liu and Vinter, 2015】; 【111†Li et al., 2013】; 【52†Niu et al., 2021】; 【53†Cong et al., 2025】; 【112†Greathouse and Daga, 2014b】; 【113†Du et al., 2022】).\nL432: TileSpMSpV is more directly related to our work: it extends tiling to GPU SpMSpV and also enables TileBFS by organizing matrices and vectors in tile form(【55†Ji et al., 2022】). Bit-GraphBLAS targets binary graph workloads with B2SR, a two-level representation that compacts block contents as bits(【114†Chen et al., 2022a】).\nL433: SpInfer uses bitmap encoding for GPU SpMM, while Sparse Register Tiling combines code transformation and data compression for CPU SpMM; both target static sparse weights with dense inputs rather than dynamic sparse vectors(【39†Fan et al., 2025】; 【115†Wilkinson et al., 2023】). These designs show the value of blocking and compact metadata.\nL434: DB-SpMSpV differs in that it targets dynamic sparse vectors and general-valued SpMSpV: it keeps block-level CSR/CSC views only at the high level, reuses one low-level block payload, and still supports both push and pull without full CSR+CSC duplication.\nL435: SpMSpV kernels and adaptivity. Early SpMSpV work addressed irregular accumulation through graph-driven execution, k-way merge, sorting, and work-efficient parallel algorithms(【68†Yang et al., 2015】; 【44†Azad and Buluç, 2017】; 【59†Li et al., 2018】).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndeep-prove/LICENSE at master · Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove · GitHub (https://github.com/Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove/blob/master/LICENSE)\nciteturn4view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view3\",\"id\":89}); Total lines: 257\n"
